==Conduct==
On election day seven Colombian security services personnel were killed and eight were missing; parallels were drawn with FARC attacks and Santos' tenure as Defense Minister.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/06/201062018745679669.html |title=Police killed on Colombia poll day - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=2010-05-29 |access-date=2010-06-23}}</ref>

== Results ==
No candidate received an outright majority in the first round vote held on 30 May. Santos and Mockus faced one another in the runoff election on 20 June, leading to the election of Juan Manuel Santos as the next Colombian President. Santos achieved a landslide victory, with 69 per cent of the votes.<ref name="Landslide win for Santos in Colombian election">{{cite news|author=Hennigan, Tom|url=http://www.irishtimes.com/newspaper/world/2010/0622/1224273028612.html|title=Landslide win for Santos in Colombian election|date=22 June 2010|newspaper=[[The Irish Times]]|publisher=Irish Times Trust|access-date=22 June 2010}}</ref> Mockus got 27.51 per cent of votes.<ref>{{cite news|author=Xuequan, Mu|url=http://news.xinhuanet.com/english2010/world/2010-06/21/c_13359750.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121106220443/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english2010/world/2010-06/21/c_13359750.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=6 November 2012|title=Santos elected as new president of Colombia|date=20 June 2010|work=[[Xinhua News Agency]]|access-date=20 June 2010}}</ref> This was the largest margin of victory for a president in the democratic period of Colombia's history.<ref name="U.S. is 'pleased' with Colombia's new president-elect Santos">{{cite news|url=http://www.thehindu.com/news/international/article478757.ece|title=U.S. is 'pleased' with Colombia's new president-elect Santos|date=22 June 2010|work=[[The Hindu]]|access-date=22 June 2010}}{{dead link|date=September 2026|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Santos won 32 of the country's 33 electoral districts.<ref name="Landslide win for Santos in Colombian election" /> His allies have an overwhelming majority in the Colombian Congress.<ref name="Landslide win for Santos in Colombian election" /> Santos vowed to continue his predecessor's hardline stance against the country's Marxist rebels.<ref name="Landslide win for Santos in Colombian election" /> He paraphrased [[Isaac Newton]] – "If we have come far it's because we are standing on the shoulders of giants" – and said he would rid Colombia of what he described as the "nightmare of violence".<ref>{{cite news|author1=Murphy, Helen |author2=Bristow, Matthew|url=http://www.businessweek.com/news/2010-06-21/colombia-s-santos-hails-uribe-in-landslide-victory-correct-.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120529202315/http://www.businessweek.com/news/2010-06-21/colombia-s-santos-hails-uribe-in-landslide-victory-correct-.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=29 May 2012|title=Colombia's Santos Hails Uribe in Landslide Victory (Correct)|date=21 June 2010|work=[[Business Week]]|access-date=21 June 2010}}</ref> The United States [[United States Department of State|State Department]] said it was "pleased" with the election of Santos and praised the "spirited debate" before the runoff and Colombia's "longstanding commitment to democratic principles".<ref name="U.S. is 'pleased' with Colombia's new president-elect Santos" />

